  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1246 PM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nA strong southwest jet aloft has brought gusty winds to the high \ncountry and the majority of the plains today. Gusty winds are likely \nto continue for the next few hours. The high country could see wind \ngusts of 50-60 mph with slightly higher gusts possible at the \nhighest elevations. We expect 30-40 mph wind gusts to continue \nacross the plains. These winds are expected to slack off in the next \nfew hours as the upper-level flow starts to weaken gradually, but \nsome gusty winds could be possible through late tonight at the \nhighest elevations. Additionally, relative humidity remains at or \nbelow 20% for most of the plains south of I-76. Thus, we have had \nsome extreme to near-critical fire weather conditions today and this \nthreat will continue for a few more hours. Today is forecast to be \nthe warmest day of the upcoming week with highs mainly in the 60s \nfor the plains. Cooler air is starting to filter into the northern \nplains and northern metro at this hour.\n\nCurrent radar shows some snow showers ongoing across the central and \nnorthern high country with visibilities briefly dropping. There \nis the chance for some light snow accumulations this afternoon \nfor the high country with the Park Range being the most favored \nlocation. Only about an additional inch or two of snow seems \nplausible as the shortwave departs. \n\nA cold front is progged to move through the area late this afternoon \nfrom north to south. This will usher in cooler temperatures for the \nweek ahead. Saturday morning, we expect an increase in QG ascent as \nthe trough moves through and moisture has slightly increased in the \nlatest modeling. We have increased cloud cover for tomorrow, which \ncould keep temperatures across the urban corridor right at or just \nabove freezing. On Saturday morning into the early afternoon, snow \nlevels drop as cold air advection continues, 700 mb temperatures \ncool, and weak upslope flow is expected. There is a low chance \n(~20%) for some light snow on the plains with about a 30% chance for \nthe southern foothills. For the urban corridor, in particular, there \nis only about a 10% chance for very light accumulations given the \nmarginal temperatures. Odds increase very slightly for the Palmer \nDivide. The central and southern high country have a higher chance \nof seeing some accumulations tomorrow morning, but only around \none inch. If the ingredients can come together, some locations in \nthe southern foothills and southern high country could get a bit \nmore accumulation.\n\nSaturday will be much cooler than today with temperatures struggling \nto reach the mid 40s for the plains. Ridging quickly moves in \nSaturday night into Sunday, but highs are forecast to only \nmoderate a few degrees on Sunday afternoon. Models and ensemble \nguidance continue to show the potential for two different systems \naffecting our area next week- one at the beginning of the week and\none towards the end of the week. Run-to-run and model-to-model \nvariability remains high, so there is very low confidence in any \nprecipitation timing/impacts. More westerly/northwesterly flow \naloft means our central and southern high country are most \nfavored, in general. Monday into early Tuesday brings the next \nchance for snow for the high country. The NBM does a good job of \ndepicting that only light accumulations are expected. Some light \nrain and/or snow could spread onto the plains on Monday night into\nTuesday morning.\n\nWe are confident that the plains will see highs in the 40s to near \n50 and lows in the low/mid 20s which will continue through most of \nnext week. These temperatures will be 5 to even 10 degrees below \naverage- the coolest weather of the season. This forecast will be \nsomething to keep tabs on with the busy travel week ahead.",
